{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778268000,"nonce":"jUB5AApBKtooVsXcK/RQnP+sHDc=","title":"Extend make_streaming_loss_fn to support cross-timestep loss terms (e.g., StateDerivativeLoss)","message":"## Motivation\n\nStateDerivativeLoss (added in feedbax 380cbd1, issue e8f3738) computes mean(||x_t - x_{t-1}||²) over a state PyTree substate, requiring access to two consecutive timesteps. The current streaming_loss.make_streaming_loss_fn only supports per-step (TargetStateLoss) and state-independent (ModelLoss) terms.\n\nConsequence: any training run that includes StateDerivativeLoss (or any other cross-timestep loss term) must use --no-streaming-loss, paying a peak-VRAM cost. For the rlrmp baseline retrain (rlrmp issue efc4d68) this is ~240 MB extra peak VRAM — acceptable on 4090 (24 GB) but not free. Future cross-timestep terms (e.g., second derivative / jerk penalty if escalated per the rlrmp escalation order) will face the same ceiling.\n\n## Goal\n\nExtend make_streaming_loss_fn to natively support cross-timestep loss terms by buffering the previous N states. New abstract base class CrossTimestepLoss with an attribute order: int = 1 declaring how many previous states it needs; the streaming loss fn keeps a rolling buffer of the appropriate depth.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Add CrossTimestepLoss abstract base.\n- Subclass StateDerivativeLoss from it.\n- Modify make_streaming_loss_fn to detect cross-timestep terms in the loss menu and adjust the buffer depth accordingly.\n- Tests: streaming-vs-batched equivalence on a small problem with StateDerivativeLoss enabled.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- Smoothness term: feedbax e8f3738.\n- rlrmp consumer: rlrmp efc4d68 (baseline-with-smoothness retrain).\n- Shahbazi et al. 2025 Eq. 1: https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.03.26.645562 — reference for the smoothness term motivation.\n\n## Deferred (out of scope for this issue)\n\nOther cross-timestep terms (jerk on output, etc.) listed in the rlrmp escalation order (efc4d68) will benefit from this extension once landed.","files":null}]}